 "ref": "A promissory note is a written I-O-U: a promise to repay a set amount by a set date. In his plea agreement, Horwitz acknowledged that one-in-M-M's notes were securities under federal law. Each note listed the amount borrowed, typically from about thirty-five thousand dollars to one and a half million. It also listed the amount to be paid back at maturity, typically six or twelve months later: a return of twenty-five to forty-five percent. In its complaint, the S-E-C alleged a slightly different range: a typical profit of thirty-five to forty-five percent over the life of the note.",
 "text": "A promissory note is a written IOU, a promise to repay a set amount by a set date. In his plea agreement, Horwitz acknowledged that one in MM's notes were securities under federal law. Each note listed the amount borrowed, typically from about thirty-five thousand dollars to one and a half million. It also listed the amount to be paid back at maturity, typically six or twelve months later, a return of twenty-five to forty-five percent. In its complaint, the SEC alleged a slightly different range, a typical profit of thirty-five to forty-five percent over the life of the note.",
